#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048357 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049201 :topic/math-academy-id 3725 :topic/title "Properties of Lines Given in Standard Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049476 :topic/math-academy-id 3979 :topic/title "Parallel and Perpendicular Lines"}] :topic/id #uuid "9835c16e-d4e3-5c8e-9410-e1bb9732900c" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110599 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110334 :question/id #uuid "4cc37ad7-b382-5b7e-bcda-92b2818c5650" :question/math-academy-id "e-14359" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $y= -\\dfrac{2}{5}x-3?$\n\n- $y = 5x + 1$\n\n- $y = \\dfrac{5}{2}x + 3$\n\n- $y = -\\dfrac{x}{2} + 5$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nThe line $y=-\\dfrac{2}{5}x-3$ is in slope-intercept form, and its slope is $-\\dfrac{2}{5}.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y = \\dfrac{5}{2}x + 3$ has a slope of $\\dfrac{5}{2}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n\\left( -\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right) \\cdot \\dfrac{5}{2} = -1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y = \\dfrac{5}{2}x + 3$ is perpendicular to the given line.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Lines in Slope-Intercept Form are Perpendicular"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110600 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110343 :question/id #uuid "b5470454-6a27-5eda-a323-74ce90259f4c" :question/math-academy-id "e-14734" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $4x - 2y = 10?$\n\n- $y = \\dfrac{1}{2}x + 3$\n\n- $y = -4x + 1$\n\n- $y = -\\dfrac{1}{2}x -5$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $4x - 2y = 10,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-2y & =10 \\\\ -2y & =-4x+10 \\\\ y & =2x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $2.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y = -\\dfrac{1}{2}x -5$ has a slope of $-\\dfrac{1}{2}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot \\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = -1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y = -\\dfrac{1}{2}x -5$ is perpendicular to the given line.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Lines in Standard Form are Perpendicular"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110601 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110335 :question/id #uuid "a53a3337-108b-5129-b203-ff70ce8d0abe" :question/math-academy-id "e-14362" :question/problem "The lines $y=2x+9$ and $3y+kx = 12$ are perpendicular. What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\n- The first line is in slope-intercept form, and the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So the slope of the line $y=2x+9$ is $2.$\n\n- To find the slope of  $3y+kx = 12,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y+kx & =12 \\\\ 3y & =-kx+12 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{k}{3}x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line $3y +kx = 12$ is $-\\dfrac{k}{3}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot\\left(- \\dfrac{k}{3}\\right) = -1\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k = \\dfrac{3}{2}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Determining Unknown Parameters Given Two Perpendicular Lines"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110602 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110336 :question/id #uuid "5eca5ef2-627f-5d32-8edb-88e223b11d71" :question/math-academy-id "e-14365" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to $x=2$ and passes through the point $(3,4).$" :question/worked-solution "Since the line $x=2$ is a vertical line, a perpendicular line would need to be horizontal. So, the perpendicular line must take the form $y=k$ for some value of $k.$\n\nThe perpendicular line needs to go through the point $(3,4),$ which has a $y$-coordinate of $4.$ So, the perpendicular line is $y=4.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equations of Perpendicular Horizontal and Vertical Lines"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1427 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050352 :topic/math-academy-id 6415 :topic/title "Slopes of Perpendicular Lines"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050473 :topic/math-academy-id 3562 :topic/title "Finding Equations of Perpendicular Lines"}] :topic/title "Perpendicular Lines in the Coordinate Plane"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048780 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050467 :topic/math-academy-id 1102 :topic/title "Problem Solving Using Vector Diagrams"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051014 :topic/math-academy-id 1105 :topic/title "Linear Combinations of Vectors and Their Properties"}] :topic/id #uuid "be10c23c-41f3-53b5-9245-d50fb098c325" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096652 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096451 :question/id #uuid "dccb5bac-e528-5b42-9949-36b3870fdde1" :question/math-academy-id "e-4287" :question/problem "Consider the picture below. Given that $\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$.\n\n![](images/b8/b8abf22d14f93a38c4d01f368b8171e4ccfe90086d932f5b58453baa46391405.png)" :question/worked-solution "![](images/76/76b9867a7c1ef484922241ebe44272f0104e406c31310bf36227a13850f50d8b.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup ONM$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $ON=2|\\mathbf{n}|$;\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{ON}$ and $\\mathbf{n}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overrightarrow{ON}=2\\mathbf{n}$.\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $NM=2|\\mathbf{k}|$\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{NM}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overrightarrow{NM}=2\\mathbf{k}$.\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\overset{\\to }{ON}+\\overset{\\to }{NM} \\\\  & =2n+2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}=2\\mathbf{n}+2\\mathbf{k}$. \n\nSince $\\mathbf{n} \\nparallel \\mathbf{k},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{n}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda=2$ and $\\mu=2$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096653 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096452 :question/id #uuid "09d593cc-526e-5691-9a18-efff515eae59" :question/math-academy-id "e-4288" :question/problem "Consider the following diagram, where $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a},$ $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf{b},$ and $X$ divides $AB$ in the ratio $3:1.$ Find $\\overrightarrow{OX}.$\n\n![](images/67/678ea2936b8a5b1ded74cb8a0875c629e8be0f7002c98492ebf2e8109fc603ef.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OX} = \\overrightarrow{OA}+\\overrightarrow{AX}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\mathbf{a},$ so we just need to work out $\\overrightarrow{AX}.$\n\nFirst, from $\\triangle OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-a+b \\\\  & =b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AX:XB=3:1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AX}=\\dfrac{3}{4}\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\dfrac{3}{4}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OX} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AX} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{3}{4}(b-a) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}a+\\frac{3}{4}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096701 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096495 :question/id #uuid "839ed5f5-165f-5de5-aba1-551ac605475f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5212" :question/problem "Consider the picture below.\n\n![](images/f2/f2518c2e998e7c7f551548a4cc7283720a66428ea8fa9872494a81cc3848ba9e.png)\n\nGiven that ![](images/c6/c6714d0a6eaa5c7d354d46e8d6d60da7f3d2011df0fc75773036cf7ae510c5e3.png), find ![](images/24/24878591902884f45f14f5c447bc326586a411a8870b205fc12825a4ab2c738a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that when two known vectors are not parallel, a linear combination of them is unique. So, once we rewrite ![](images/5c/5c644b15efcf5a4274de23242a722604ead2ff800ea29ba7a650729227fefdf0.png) in terms of ![](images/27/27f36b1d1846e12e3c50b7d4e47f5745727792d52a48e4d85b689ec36e3d25a0.png) and ![](images/a7/a7b9033c421072576aaf959980d778c319a8ed0f849e08db644b63bc6994088c.png) we can match coefficients to determine the unknown scalar.\n\nTo find ![](images/16/166c90a9c29f99347bff6b397f31d705ec6407a19b4d71246f98308bbd7a5473.png) we want to match the expression\n![](images/fc/fc79367916dd7671c17de4f40b3d83f91d41c2f9988645964de0b53e5b11ab9b.png)\n\nWe are given that one of the components is ![](images/91/91f686c6ca02e85c386cb0f9e80b66beface446d3a541952e0ac91738ad0db37.png) So, we first draw the vector ![](images/d5/d54b9a8919c7b6106fcf5770c62b1823605d7be98a55d5333e73bcfb7528997a.png) from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point ![](images/6e/6e1bbbe9eb66856824ff70d16cc2c077519d361546b226a67146e20f15a24dd0.png)\n\n![](images/ea/ea517ac4ca8e0bcf7ba48f23e66f100323d5485f1221219aacfbea9148f4a1af.png)\n\nNote that in ![](images/f1/f1c944e6afa4e9cd84bc68004a2bfd918d81498a727aeb7c56ec627806bf93f6.png), we have:\n\n- ![](images/b7/b7ac390c4e43b2fa2fbb4063632f98869256cea877caa5e5dad4aad33ae94722.png)\n\n- ![](images/7e/7e5f2b01647ab01052fff18ab760273eebe19d47b56051b1953adf587d7f9fb3.png) and ![](images/78/784225d11bf94b7fbd67f8e2395695141eb75b1cb44f89d49b29bb3a2031534b.png) have the same direction\n\nHence, ![](images/21/21261c8ad0d86a9e7300ffc58c57fd76beef9f627492bcfe2051d5e3cfd82a4e.png)\n\nAdditionally,\n![](images/ae/ae9c98ded3aa4317a729571710ad38d973a2d67b5faeb9ad26af7c24ee5c3193.png)\n\nConsequently, ![](images/ae/aee3287ad64a654880216142756811bd4d5232b3dba2f48378b8b1c5241e4316.png)\n\nSince ![](images/46/466863f8c7522ac89896a4233a823be02940535097c28328fa2c9e9e1f4bc3d8.png) the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of ![](images/37/3720c5887713d3f7a29be384fe5e883390935b2b9b469af626a8dba968f4da8b.png) we get:\n![](images/4a/4ac145b710c5354b44a4ea1dc820d70f54b5c17985f078fce6313b671ec26fd9.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235285 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234976 :question/id #uuid "aeaa36a2-41b5-5b7b-9797-f39df1e34050" :question/math-academy-id "e-26144" :question/problem "Consider the picture below. \n\n![](images/d4/d4f18c3567d0c55d3d6e002fbe2c6ac50b97b7adc14b016c8e95006de98ca15d.png)\n\nGiven that ![](images/a7/a77e5fef143d51d984ce41363465af5f210291f6355b59da4302a944b6a49197.png) find ![](images/8a/8aef212f0b07b6c0fb04e3b3c0e415f3c93d698d48414bc0860a6405db20099b.png)" :question/worked-solution "To find ![](images/ae/ae4e47577b23025037443e79415dc50ff81b984cac5c4bb82421f985cfe4d69b.png) we want to match the expression\n![](images/bd/bd1ef582b76affe848465f42a9c78e897d93f1a2b535b7e880da8ab016561451.png)\n\nWe are given that one of the components is ![](images/dc/dc10081b11a8cfba96ed4d16d9ca1c6448af7f6198a5550955c2d123a657d339.png) So, we first draw the vector ![](images/7e/7efe1d8a24967d9509e73d84e1fb73eea1df1c2f113360cd3a34d8ef29df62e9.png) from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point ![](images/06/06046901b5ecb50bfedb17ffaf4c19cf690e50bd2e6e7c668fa59787ce4d71b7.png)\n\n![](images/65/65a6b5743ab9b6ef820744d0d6601b7dfd8de08a3f895661d96d2fe2cab8e0a7.png)\n\nLet ![](images/49/492123d15daf8306f0f5f893c7230d60f9eb8312d17b20f9a331f40f36b69b48.png) be the endpoint of the vector ![](images/d8/d8c5a62b30eeb1bc2e35d95dfc5d964dd00e512f3f7b1b53379755b1ebff5129.png) Then\n\n![](images/2d/2d7c2df783579edf0ca151150eeb55471b3f3fc2afc4e7836678b25670b484c4.png)\n\nIn ![](images/3e/3e50bb6908d0311cbf9fe09b7a8039c9d438eeda7d312f7fa87d6aa835523d49.png) we have:\n\n- ![](images/9a/9a61fb0fb1ada64abe8bd22d8b6f14a8248212de90bff583ebd1881bdf21efc5.png)\n\n- ![](images/af/af03af84c1a16b4d534cc63ac9a1b3d8d5b7d181b32cb23190deb192d4d8b8b1.png) and ![](images/d9/d9dc9bcbe34586fe4e368536ed74377c765274e2e5b008e1fee5d65e3b5008c0.png) have opposite directions\n\nHence,\n![](images/da/da5024a9515dce187468fd87b63acfd0fd89bfd867a9f03388782221604a3e74.png)\n\nSimilarly,\n\n- ![](images/cb/cb4b9bd2f50fd4b2da78369b90c522bc2b7ee20cbafa9460b643889733487c5b.png)\n\n- ![](images/5f/5fdd95564252a9a4218055ca7670a535233aa026955b7e410700e26c4f7d170c.png) and ![](images/1f/1f42d4df263db3845ca2361b8f89c4075e0cdb9b39e5564c39ae82115932a201.png) have opposite directions\n\nHence,\n![](images/da/da69cf085ea0816397356abf0878c798ab653c04fe34a51b8b849610d57778d4.png)\n\nAdditionally,\n\n![](images/57/5765a85c90552570f9a2a041f2f324a9fa0ce183d42708344531a13fbee802c8.png)\n\nConsequently,\n![](images/57/578802d62c9ab4cfbc8f250975b5b7be1396907593ba95901b8b21ad6a116d04.png)\n\nSince ![](images/46/466863f8c7522ac89896a4233a823be02940535097c28328fa2c9e9e1f4bc3d8.png) the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of ![](images/37/3720c5887713d3f7a29be384fe5e883390935b2b9b469af626a8dba968f4da8b.png) we get\n\n![](images/1c/1c2480355dc4e29a136a3bd7b6855e076f1c9698507523cafbd934521b91c6bf.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor When Direction Reverses"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1106 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049485 :topic/math-academy-id 1376 :topic/title "The Vector Equation of a Line"}] :topic/title "Describing the Position Vector of a Point Using Known Vectors"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049991 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049075 :topic/math-academy-id 1573 :topic/title "Graphing Secant and Cosecant"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050172 :topic/math-academy-id 3540 :topic/title "Describing Properties of the Sine Function"}] :topic/id #uuid "245d7b8c-ac58-53e1-bbb6-af3096067f47" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176053 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175699 :question/id #uuid "12dc1c50-69bc-54d8-8d60-a7fc144c9644" :question/math-academy-id "e-11008" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the graph of $y=\\csc x?$\n\n- The function takes the value $1$ at $x = -\\dfrac {\\pi} 2 + 2 \\pi n,$ where $n$ is an integer\n\n- The range of the function is $(-\\infty,+\\infty)$\n\n- The function has zeroes at $x = 0, \\pm \\pi, \\pm 2 \\pi,\\dots$" :question/worked-solution "Let's have a look at the graph of $y=\\csc x.$\n\n![](images/a9/a92d46319d381f0a8cab7823021b3b7e9b5c030ad964586b8e72129a2cd5abe7.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- The cosecant takes the value $1$ at $x = \\dfrac {\\pi} 2 + 2 \\pi n.$ Therefore, statement I is false.\n\n- The range of $y = \\csc x$ is $(-\\infty,-1] \\cup [1, \\infty).$ Therefore, statement II is false.\n\n- The cosecant graph does not intersect the $x$-axis. Therefore, statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, none of the statements are true."} :knowledge-point/title "Describing the General Properties of the Cosecant Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176054 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175700 :question/id #uuid "a1713375-241f-5a53-a109-3c3d0f917411" :question/math-academy-id "e-11009" :question/problem "If $\\csc x = 8,$ then what is $\\csc(x-8\\pi)?$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y = \\csc x$ is $2\\pi.$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\csc{x} = \\csc{(x+2n\\pi)} .\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-4$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+2\\cdot (-4)\\cdot \\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-8\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\csc{x} = 8,$ then $\\csc{(x-8\\pi)} = 8.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Cosecant Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176060 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175706 :question/id #uuid "12eb5678-b62b-50ad-97bf-88768c4bf365" :question/math-academy-id "e-11015" :question/problem "If $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{7}{2},$ then find the value of $\\csc(-x - 20\\pi).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y = \\csc{x}.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8e7894ea970c539ca59a73713fa15450f4361b3fc8bc22183e3fe84946411bd.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\csc{x}$ is $2\\pi.$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\csc{x}$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\csc(-x) = -\\csc{x}.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\csc{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x-20\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x-2\\pi \\cdot 10) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\csc{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x) & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\csc(-x - 20\\pi) = -\\dfrac{7}{2}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Oddness Property of the Cosecant Function"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3567 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048666 :topic/math-academy-id 1258 :topic/title "General Solutions of Elementary Trigonometric Equations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049372 :topic/math-academy-id 778 :topic/title "Properties of Transformed Secant and Cosecant Functions"}] :topic/title "Describing Properties of the Cosecant Function"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050523 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049155 :topic/math-academy-id 1493 :topic/title "Graphing Tangent and Cotangent"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049873 :topic/math-academy-id 725 :topic/title "Even and Odd Functions"}] :topic/id #uuid "5049bff8-1875-5713-9a67-c3aa9836de2c" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176049 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175695 :question/id #uuid "2f4571af-99cf-5a4a-9998-fb6a0f7d6be9" :question/math-academy-id "e-11004" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\tan x?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The zeros of the function are at $x = \\pi n.$\n\n- The function has asymptotes at $x = 2\\pi n.$\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in [-1,1].$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\tan x.$\n\n![](images/32/326418398ce1a33f03a21f3bdb58107856f4ccb72241429f1604486281e60822.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The zeros occur at $x=0, \\pm \\pi, \\pm2\\pi,\\ldots,$ which can be written as $x=n\\pi.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function has an asymptote at $x = \\dfrac \\pi 2$ and the period is $\\pi.$ Therefore, the asymptotes occur at $x = \\dfrac \\pi 2 + \\pi n.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function takes values on all the real numbers, hence the range is $y\\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Tangent Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176050 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175696 :question/id #uuid "f4649856-d777-5468-a7f4-df50479e36d7" :question/math-academy-id "e-11005" :question/problem "If $\\tan\\left( x\\right) = -2$ for some value of $x,$ then find the value of $\\tan\\left( x-2\\pi\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=\\tan x$ is $\\pi.$ Therefore, for any integer $n,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\tan x = \\tan (x+n\\pi).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡x & =tan⁡(x+(-2)\\cdot \\pi )=tan⁡(x-2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\tan\\left( x\\right) = -2,$ we get that $\\tan\\left( x-2\\pi\\right) = -2.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Tangent Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176058 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175704 :question/id #uuid "0b146642-914c-52ce-9939-86752e95dcd2" :question/math-academy-id "e-11013" :question/problem "If $\\tan x = -\\dfrac{2}{3},$ find the value of $\\tan (-x+7\\pi).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\tan{x}.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\tan{x}$ is $\\pi.$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\tan{x}$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\tan(-x) = -\\tan{x}.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\tan{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x+7\\pi ) & =tan⁡(-x+\\pi \\cdot 7) \\\\  & =tan⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\tan{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x) & =-tan⁡x \\\\  & =-(-\\frac{2}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\tan (-x+7\\pi)=\\dfrac{2}{3}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Oddness Property of the Tangent Function"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3558 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050427 :topic/math-academy-id 3566 :topic/title "Describing Properties of the Cotangent Function"}] :topic/title "Describing Properties of the Tangent Function"}]}
